    ":question/id": "99f3a3dc-a29b-56b3-b6b2-eb00c09e4e97",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-24442",
    ":question/problem": "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)=p$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=q$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y})=$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the quotient rule for common logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for common logarithms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(y) \\\\  & =p-q\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
